{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Polyrith"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.base_point_root_lt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.base_point_root_lt_one","kind":"theorem","line":202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L202-L209","doc":"**The base point.** At `φ = 0` the reduced cubic is the 5₂ polynomial, whose\nreal root is `< 1`; so `1 − x·y = 1 − y > 0` there too.\n\n`u = y − 1` turns it into `u³ + u² + 2u + 1`, which is `≥ 1` for `u ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.xy_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.xy_neg","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L148-L200","doc":"**No factor reaches the cut on `0 < φ < π`.**\n\n`hre` and `him` are the real and imaginary parts of `P(y) = 0` for\n`P(Y) = Y³ − 2cY² + (1+2c)Y − 1` at `y = t·(c − is)` — that is, at the root whose\nproduct with `x = c + is` is the real number `t`. Given `s ≠ 0` and `c² + s² = 1`,\nthe conclusion is `t < 0`.\n\nSince the factor in question is `1 − x·y = 1 − t`, this says it is `> 1`: not\nmerely off `(−∞, 0]` but bounded away from it. The same statement covers\n`1 − y/x` by `s ↦ −s`, which the hypotheses are invariant under. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.quintic_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.quintic_pos","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L134-L144","doc":"**The quintic is positive on `t > 0`.** Two cases, neither needing its\nminimum: for `t ≤ 1` it is `t⁵ + (1 − t³)`, and for `t > 1` it exceeds `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.eliminant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.eliminant","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L121-L132","doc":"**The eliminant.** The two real equations force\n`t³ (t−1)⁴ (t⁵ − t³ + 1) = 0`.\n\n`linear_combination` checks the Bézout identity, so the resultant computation is\nverified here rather than imported from a CAS. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.reduced_at_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.reduced_at_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L113-L117","doc":"At `c = −1` (the endpoint `x = −1`) it is the negative of the Watson cubic\n`w³ − 2w² − w + 1` under `y ↦ −w` — which is why the endpoint factor is `1 − w₁`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.reduced_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.reduced_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L109-L111","doc":"At `c = 1` (the base point `x = 1`) the reduced cubic is the 5₂ polynomial. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.add_inv_eq_two_re","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.add_inv_eq_two_re","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L102-L105","doc":"**On the unit circle the coefficients become real.** `x + 1/x = 2·Re x`, so\nthe monic cubic above is `Y³ − 2cY² + (1+2c)Y − 1` with `c = Re x = cos φ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.ggm_eq_reduced","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoCutAvoidance.ggm_eq_reduced","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoCutAvoidance.lean#L95-L100","doc":"The GGM curve, divided by its leading coefficient. Off `x = 0` the roots in\n`y` are exactly the roots of the monic cubic on the right. ","signatureChunk":0}]}